A||N||3||FullRaceCam||Shifting Problems||||1||
Z||000000||FullRaceCam||09-12-2001||05:24 PM||||  I am having problems and now have a computer so I can call for help. <BR>  Two years ago, I changed the clutch in my '88 Formula. It slipped in high gear under acceleration. No big deal, I followed the Haynes manual plus V8 Archies method and it all went fine. <BR>  About six months later, the throwout bearing started making noises ( high pitched buzzing, rattling) when the clutch was depressed. I figured that the bearing was bad or poor original quality. Don't remember the name of the kit or bearing brand. Something I wasn't familiar with. Anyway, changed it out and noise went away.<BR>  Now the darned thing won't shift. It left me stuck in traffic because I couldn't get it in any gear. Turned motor off, put in first, motor wouldn't turn over. Rolled back, tried again and now I could shift. <BR>  Got a friend, rebled clutch system(Archies method), adjusted shift cables(Haynes method) and no go. Motor off, I can row shifter through gears no problem. Start car and I can find Third, fourth and fifth without much trouble. Second is a little harder. First I can't engage at all. Same for reverse. <BR>   Oh yeah, 1) When installing the throwout brg, both times I thought the fit was loose. I asked at parts shop and dealer if a sleeve was available - told no. I never measured the clearance at the time, so all I have is my memory that the fit should have been closer. I don't recall if the output shaft was scored or not. I didn't grease it on assembly.( not that I recall). 2) Now, at times with the shifting the pedal seems really stiff, something will click/snap in the back, and then the pedal is free. <BR>   Surfing leads me to think that the clutch fork is binding. I don't think the clutch assembly should need replacing.<BR>   My slave cylinder travel is only 3/4 to 1 inch and I don't think I'm gonna get any more. The master is only abut three years old and the slave I had to use a kit. I think thats about all the travel it had before and after.<BR>  Are these problems common, Am I on the right track? I will be pulling it apart soon but would like to see if replacements are available up here before ordering from south of border.  Do the syncros wear out? How do I know from Type 1 or Type 2 clutch fork? Is splitting the case a big deal to replace that output shaft bearing. I've never done it and trannys are a forbidden zone to me. Dragons be there.<BR>   Any comments would be most welcome.<BR>  Sincerely, FRC, B.C. Canada.<P> PS. Deepest condolences for the terrible tragedy yesterday.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||09-12-2001||10:46 PM||||Goto <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A>  for instructions on splitting the case on a Getrag tranny.  I've done it, and it's a piece of cake.  Really.  It's just intimidating cuz it's an unknown.  You said your throwout bearing clearance looked loose - how's the input shaft bearing on the tranny look?  There is a sleeve made into the bearing that the throwout bearing rides on.  This sleeve will break off of the input shaft bearing and cause the chattering noise you describe (that's how my clutch went in my Formula).  A replacement input shaft bearing/sleeve is available from The Fiero Store, <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A>  .  I tried to get one from NAPA, but it didn't have the sleeve on the bearing.  I suspect this may have been your problem.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Voytek||09-13-2001||03:35 PM||||As far as the clutch travel (slave cylinder), I would check the simple things first.  Did you remember to bleed the clutch slave cylinder?  I've had a similar problem once.  It took me quite a while to figure out that just bleeding the line by deppressing the pedal was not enough.  You also need to push the rod back into the slave cylinder before you tighten the nipple.<P>If you eliminate that, it's also possible that either the slave or (more likely) the master cylinder is bad.  I have an '87 GT and I installed a new master cylinder a couple of years ago.  It was about 18 months later that I needed a new clutch (or so I thought).  In any case, I got a new clutch put in but the shop told me the line doesn't hold enough pressure to push it and they figure the MC is toast.  I did NOT believe them.  I made them go and check everything for a day and half.  Finally I gave in and supplied my own new MC.  Fixed the problem instantly.  And you couldn't even  tell that the old one didn't work.<P>Finally, I can offer one more experience.  As I mentioned, I thought I needed a new clutch.  There was a noise coming from the engine in the form of fast (at speed of RPM) rattling/clicking/ticking every time I deppressed the clutch pedal.  My first reaction - clutch.  So I went and had it replaced.  The noise went away for almost a year.  Then it came back.  I was ready to sue the shop that did the clutch (they did not want to warranty it).  Then I took it to a different shop.  It turned out that it was a crank bearing!  The bearing was faulty and every time the clutch was disengaged, the crankshaft travelled just slightly back and forth.  Needless to say, I've had the engine rebuilt.  20,000 kms later there is no noise.<P>Just some things to think about.<BR>I don't know much about the release bearing or forks (other than the fact that I had all that replaced when I got the new clutch).<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FullRaceCam||09-15-2001||01:14 AM||||  Thanks for the responses. I know from the previous bearings I bought that they didn't have sleeves. I'll go to the better source. Also, I was trying to provide as much info as possible but forgot to mention that the car is a daily driver doing the long commute. It has 250k ( 156000 miles) and is getting a little tired. I'll change the master cylinder first( cheapest/easiest). If that's not the solution, then drop the motor and look inside again. This time I'll split the case and replace the input shaft brg. It's getting close to the time to park it in the garage and start a major overhaul of everything. I like the 3800 idea.<BR>   Once again, thanks for the help. I don't post a lot(twice) but I read all the time. Great info and ideas. I'll be back when the time is right.<BR>   Sincerely, FRC.||||reg||1
